Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:51:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 27/40] Neighbour flags: no spin above the performance-core
 count (snapshot cg29)

A wait for a neighbour flag spun like the barriers (OPENEMS_BARRIER_SPIN_US, default 50 us) before it slept. With
more threads than performance cores, some threads run on the efficiency cores of the Apple M5: the waits for them
are long, and a spinning thread only takes power and a core from the others. SetupFused() now sets the spin of the
flag waits from the thread count (FlagSpinTime()): none (the loop's first 128 checks, then sleep) when the threads
outnumber the performance cores (hw.perflevel0.physicalcpu), the barrier spin time up to that count and on other
CPUs (not measured there). OPENEMS_FLAGS_SPIN_US sets it for any thread count; verbose > 1 prints it with the
fused-sweep line. Scheduling only (bit-identical); the stress run of the flags commit (every wait that is not
satisfied at once sleeps, at 3, 8 and 10 threads and the automatic count) ran this path.

Paired interleaved A/B in one build, OPENEMS_BARRIER_SPIN_US=0 against the default 50 us, 4 rounds, median paired
ratio, other processes on 0.3-2 cores (patch x5 at 4 and 6 threads: 5-6 cores):
  model            4 thr   6 thr   8 thr   10 thr  automatic count
  GCPW line        x1.065  x1.064  x1.061  x1.066  x1.054
  notch tutorial   x1.008  x1.054  x0.976  x1.095  x1.105
  SMA launch       x0.989  x1.099  x1.018  x1.021  x0.999
  patch tutorial   x1.013  x1.068  x1.061  x1.004  x1.252
  patch x5         x0.991  x1.017  x1.004  x1.001  x1.013
  notch x3         x0.978  x1.034  x0.987  x1.061  x1.036
(the notch tutorial at 8 threads ran under 2 cores of other load, its rounds spread from x0.75 to x1.22). At 4
threads, all on performance cores, sleeping at once gains nothing outside the noise and costs notch x3 2 % in every
round, so the spin stays up to the performance-core count.
This build against the one before (the default against 50 us; other processes on 0.3-0.5 cores, 4 rounds):
GCPW line x1.088 [1.086-1.133] at 8 threads and x1.123 [1.049-1.163] with the automatic count, notch tutorial
x1.094 [1.090-1.107] and x1.051 [1.044-1.057]; 4 threads unchanged (x0.999, x0.998).

